=== REASONING ===
Why a merge contract instead of "L2 wins":
  Per phase-38-00 bug-fix Change 1 and ADR-007, dropping legacy or stale L2
  values silently zeroes user-facing dashboards. The intent is that
  IsLiveSignalFresh is an INFORMATIONAL freshness signal, not a delete
  filter on the read path. Both layers must contribute to the merged
  result, and the value with the newer non-zero Timestamp wins per signal.
  Legacy zero-Timestamp values are returned but lose to any non-zero
  Timestamp. Ties on identical non-zero Timestamps prefer L2 (cross-pod
  authoritative cache).

Why GetSignal must merge too:
  R5 of the contract: GetSignal must consult both L1 and L2 and apply the
  same per-signal merge rule, not "L2-or-fall-back-to-L1". The previous
  code returned (nil, nil) for stale L2 values without ever consulting L1.

Why LiveSignalReadLocal stays L1-only:
  Telemetry/FSM/session hot paths cannot tolerate Redis latency or failure
  modes. The local-preference branch short-circuits before any redisCache()
  invocation. Verified by T6 (failingRedisSignalClient + LiveSignalReadLocal
  succeeds; mode=local + LiveSignalReadDistributed also succeeds).

Why Redis errors still surface:
  R7 of the contract: empty L2 result triggers L1 fallback, but L2 ERROR
  surfaces wrapped via fmt.Errorf("...%w"). T7 asserts errors.Is preserves
  the sentinel.

Why freshSignalValues is deleted, not retained:
  The prompt body: "Remove freshSignalValues if it has no remaining
  callers; otherwise keep it but stop calling it from GetAll/GetSignal."
  After the GetAll edit removes the only call site, the helper has zero
  callers in repo (grep verified). Keeping dead code violates anti-laziness
  guideline. IsLiveSignalFresh is the surviving freshness oracle and
  remains in redis_cache.go untouched.

Tie-break to L2 on equal timestamps:
  signal.Store always stamps Timestamp = time.Now() so practical
  collisions are rare; deterministic tie-break to L2 prevents flakiness in
  test fixtures that build identical timestamps. L2 is also the cross-pod
  authoritative cache per ADR-007.

Existing test compatibility:
  TestHybridLiveSignalStoreDistributedReadsPreferRedisWhenAvailable still
  passes under the new merge rule because the test sequence (L1 update
  followed by L2 update) gives L2 a strictly later timestamp, so L2 wins
  by the merge rule, not by unconditional preference. A clarifying comment
  is added, not a rename, to keep diff scope minimal.

